id: pm-cha95z
title: "Diagnostic output budget: errors, warnings, and recovery bundles are the output an agent reads most and the only output with no declared ceiling"
description: "The token-budget work covers command results. Diagnostics are exempt, and they should not be: a failing command is precisely when an agent re-reads output, retries, and burns context, and pm's diagnostics are among its most verbose output — recovery bundles enumerate flags, validation surfaces enumerate warning classes, and usage errors reprint surrounding help. The recovery bundle defect already filed shows the failure mode is not merely size but noise that misleads. Give diagnostics the same contract every other output has: a declared ceiling, a deterministic degradation order that drops the least actionable content first, and an assertion that the single most actionable element — what to run next — is never the element dropped. The ordering principle is that a diagnostic must lead with the corrective action and only then explain, because an agent that reads the first lines and retries must have already received the fix."
type: Task
status: in_progress
priority: 2
tags[5]: agent-ux,"area:cli",diagnostics,error-contract,token-efficiency
created_at: "2026-07-25T12:28:06.310Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-21T23:59:01.664Z"
assignee: "harness:codex"
claim_principal: "harness:codex#7b28a00954f9d51f10384882"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 300
acceptance_criteria: "Errors, warnings, validation summaries, and recovery bundles each declare a default token ceiling in contract data alongside command output budgets; Degradation order is declared and deterministic, and a test asserts the corrective action survives truncation at the smallest permitted budget; Diagnostics lead with the corrective action before explanation, asserted structurally rather than by review; The measured token cost of the ten most frequent failure paths is recorded before and after as evidence on this item; A diagnostic that exceeds its declared ceiling fails the token-cost regression gate the same way command output does"
goal: project management = context management
objective: "Every diagnostic is bounded, action-first, and deterministic under truncation"
value: Failure paths are the most expensive context an agent consumes because they are read twice and often followed by a retry; leaving them unbudgeted concedes the worst case
impact: "Applies the existing budget machinery to a surface it deliberately skipped; may shorten familiar error text, so the corrective element must be explicitly protected"
outcome: "A failing command costs an agent a predictable, small number of tokens and tells it what to run next in the first lines"
parent: pm-mpbb
risk: low
confidence: 75
